children.push(...figure("fig3_truthgate.png", "Figure 3. Frozen external κ truth-gate: ΔMCIW0 vs true B for the oracle (upper bound), the frozen κ_pooled = 0.158, the naive fixed κ = 0.5, and the Egger-gated variant (Regime A). κ_pooled tracks the oracle at plausible B and over-corrects only mildly at B = 0; the gated variant is inert everywhere (the presence-gate limit)."));

children.push(H2("3.5 The honest residual: presence, not magnitude — and why it is irreducible here"));
children.push(P("A fixed external κ still over-corrects at true B=0 (κ_pooled +0.053, κ_slope +0.145; Table 4). This is not a magnitude error but the orthogonal selection-presence question: a static class-level λ cannot certify that a particular estimate within a selection-prone class happens to be unbiased. Only in-data evidence could, so we added the only such gate available — a pooled-network Egger test across all placebo-relative direct contrasts (pooling across treatments for power the per-contrast funnel lacks), applying the external κ only when |t| ≥ 1.64. Its fire-rate is 0.00–0.04 regardless of true B (Table 4, right column): it is blind in Regime A (registry selection is SE-orthogonal by construction) and underpowered in Regime B (≈ 20 studies, 4–6 per treatment). Gating therefore removes the B=0 over-correction but also removes every B > 0 win — the gated estimator ties the unadjusted NMA everywhere. This demonstrates, rather than asserts, that the presence question is irreducible on a network this sparse."));

children.push(H2("3.6 Generalisation to a second real network (depression, log-odds scale)"));
children.push(P("To test the method beyond diabetes we applied it end-to-end to a second, independent real network in a different domain and effect scale: dat.linde2015 (metadat), 66 antidepressant RCTs for major depression with arms grouped by class (SSRI, SNRI, TCA, atypical [NaSSa/SARI], MAOI [rMAO-A], plus NRI and Hypericum), outcome = treatment response, reduced to pairwise log-odds-ratio contrasts and fitted with the same engine. The network is sensible (9 treatments, 59 studies, τ = 0.18, I² = 0%; Hypericum/SNRI/TCA/SSRI all beat placebo, OR ≈ 1.7–1.9). We overlaid the real depression-class registry λ and applied the diabetes-frozen external κ = 0.158 as a cross-domain transfer test. On the truth-gate (Table 5), the registry corrections again beat every internal funnel model — PET-PEESE is catastrophic (ΔMCIW0 ≈ +1.2 at every B, worse than on diabetes because log-OR contrasts on few studies are even noisier); trim-and-fill harms; Henmi–Copas is inert. The oracle wins at B ≥ 0.15 and is exactly inert at B = 0, and the diabetes-frozen κ transfers: it improves matched-coverage error at every B, including a mild B = 0 gain (−0.042). That B = 0 behaviour is the reverse of diabetes (+0.05 cost) and is a network-specific bias–variance effect — here the low-λ classes also happen to have small placebo-relative effects, so registry-prescribed shrinkage toward the null reduces tail error rather than adding bias; the oracle remains exactly inert as designed. The core claims — registry beats internal funnel methods, PET catastrophic, external κ deployable and transferable — reproduce on a second real network."));
children.push(captionP("Table 5. ", "Truth-gate on the second real network (dat.linde2015, response log-OR; matched-coverage ΔMCIW0 vs unadjusted NMA). 'ext (diab κ=0.158)' applies the diabetes-frozen external magnitude to the depression network (cross-domain transfer). Source: linde_nma_result.json."));
children.push(tableFrom(
  ["true B", "oracle", "ext (diab κ=0.158)", "fixed κ=0.5", "PET", "TF", "HC"],
  [
    ["0.00", "+0.000 tie", { t: "−0.042 WINS", bold: true }, "−0.025 tie", "+1.224 HARMS", "+0.044 HARMS", "+0.000 tie"],
    ["0.15", "−0.062 WINS", { t: "−0.065 WINS", bold: true }, "−0.088 WINS", "+1.195 HARMS", "+0.024 HARMS", "+0.000 tie"],
    ["0.30", "−0.133 WINS", { t: "−0.084 WINS", bold: true }, "−0.146 WINS", "+1.174 HARMS", "+0.046 HARMS", "+0.000 tie"],
  ], [1080, 1480, 1720, 1440, 1360, 1300, 980]));
children.push(P("", { after: 120 }));

// 4 Discussion
children.push(H1("4. Discussion"));
children.push(P([B("What is new. "), T("Publication-bias correction in NMA has lacked a usable magnitude: the internal funnel is too noisy on a few studies per contrast (confirmed here by PET-PEESE's catastrophic failure and by a companion internal-κ̂ experiment that also failed). This paper supplies the magnitude from outside the network, using the registry's own record of which trials were published. The registered-versus-published effect gap both validates the severity ordering (no oracle; r = 0.50) and calibrates the strength (κ_pooled matches the oracle at plausible selection levels).")]));
children.push(P([B("Definitions and denominators (confidence flags). "), T("\"Published\" is operationalised as a PubMed linkage in AACT (DERIVED or RESULT reference type); \"registered-only\" as posted results without such a linkage. This is a conservative, noisy proxy for the Turner/Rising contrast: DERIVED links are auto-generated, so some genuinely published trials without an added citation fall into \"registered-only\", attenuating the gap toward zero — the true selection magnitude is therefore at least as large as reported. Denominators are modest (464 analyses; per-class published n = 34–135, registered-only n = 3–79), so per-class κ_MD is used only where min(n) ≥ 8 and the deployed estimate is the pooled scale, not per-class values. Sulfonylurea (n_reg = 3) is flagged and excluded. All effects are absolute mean differences on a common NGSP-% scale; direction is not used, so the estimate is a magnitude, not a signed effect.")]));
children.push(P([B("Deployment rule. "), T("Apply κ_pooled·(1 − λₜ) where selection is a priori expected — a selection-prone class flagged by a low registry λ — where the correction is oracle-matching. Accept the bounded +0.05 MCIW0 over-correction as the honest cost of operating without a presence gate. Do not apply it as a blanket adjustment to networks with no prior reason to suspect selection.")]));
children.push(P([B("Generalisation. "), T("The core results are not diabetes-specific: they reproduce on a second real network in a different domain and effect scale (depression, response log-odds; §3.6), where the registry corrections again beat every internal funnel model, PET-PEESE is again catastrophic, and the diabetes-frozen external κ transfers as a beneficial correction. The external validation (published-versus-registered gap tracking registry severity) also reproduces in the antidepressant domain on a scale-invariant measure.")]));
children.push(P([B("Transportability to a target population. "), T("A separate layer standardises the network to a defined target population via an external covariate (e.g. World Bank diabetes prevalence) using g-computation (Dahabreh 2020). In our known-truth simulations this layer is exactly inert when the target equals the donor pool and wins at a far target once a real effect-modifier exists, but dat.senn2013 carries no per-trial population covariate, so we make no real-data transportability claim here; it is reported as scoped simulation only.")]));
children.push(P([B("Second-domain replication (antidepressants). "), T("We tested endpoint-generalisation directly in Turner's own domain (major depression). Raw AACT HAM-D mean differences do not transfer — but this is a scale artefact, not a real boundary: raw HAM-D mixes 17/21/24-item versions, within- vs between-arm, and timepoints (41% have |MD| < 0.5, implausible), so the ratio reflects scale heterogeneity. Re-run on scale-invariant measures the gap reproduces. On the standardised significance measure z = |MD|/SE (HAM-D + MADRS), corr(κ_z, 1−λ) = +0.64 over the three adequately-powered classes (diabetes reference +0.50), and trial-level bootstrap shows published trials have robustly larger standardised effects in the two best-powered classes (SSRI κ_z +0.69 [+0.23, +1.29]; atypical +1.18 [+0.73, +1.86]; both exclude 0), while the lowest-severity class SNRI is null (−0.33 [−0.55, +0.26]) — consistent with the severity-tracking model. On response/remission log-odds the SSRI gap is also robustly positive (+0.47 [+0.09, +1.14]). We do not over-claim: only 2–3 antidepressant classes carry adequate registered-only support, so the class-level correlation is suggestive, not inferential; the robust evidence is the per-class trial-level gaps. Diabetes (7 classes) remains the primary validation; antidepressants corroborate it in a second, independent domain.")]));
children.push(P([B("Limitations. "), T("(i) The external-magnitude construction is endpoint-matched, so generalisation requires re-estimating κ on a standardised effect scale per endpoint family (raw multi-version rating scales must first be reduced to z or a log-odds effect, as above). (ii) The simulation uses the senn2013 geometry with an assumed multiplicative selection model; the oracle is an upper bound, not a claim about real senn2013 selection. (iii) λ and κ are drug-class properties from a single AACT snapshot; a live pipeline should re-freeze both per snapshot. (iv) The presence-gate limitation is intrinsic to sparse networks, not to this estimator.")]));

// 5 Conclusion
children.push(H1("5. Conclusion"));
children.push(P("For networks too sparse for internal funnel methods, a trial registry supplies both the direction and — via the registered-versus-published effect gap — a calibrated magnitude for publication-bias correction. The external magnitude matches an oracle at realistic selection levels and beats every internal selection model, while the one residual (distinguishing an unbiased estimate within a selection-prone class) is shown to be irreducible on a sparse network rather than a defect of the method."));

// References
children.push(H1("References"));
